    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside play in **Corbin Carroll** (74.78), who benefits from a favorable matchup against Landen Roupp. While Roupp possesses an average xERA (4.06), the synergy of **Corbin Carroll** batting second and the aggressive potential for stolen bases\u2014even against a competent catcher\u2014makes him our clear priority. **Austin Riley** (63.51) also grades out as an elite start, taking full advantage of a \"get-right\" matchup against Garrett, whose inflated 9.71 xERA is the most exploitable factor on the board today. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** (64.71) is a mandatory start; despite the platoon disadvantage against Anthony Kay, the sheer discrepancy in pitcher skill provides a massive boost to his floor, effectively neutralizing the lefty-lefty concerns.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Vinnie Pasquantino** (39.37), who narrowly missed the starting cut due to our strict 40.0 \"Below Zebras\" threshold. While he remains a strong asset, his score was suppressed by a difficult matchup against Ranger Suarez and unfavorable wind conditions. Conversely, we are tracking significant weather risks for **Travis Bazzana** (58.76), **Kyle Karros** (56.82), and **Mickey Moniak** (60.42), all of whom face a 36% to 45% chance of rain. **Mickey Moniak** remains in the lineup despite his poor platoon splits against Tyler Alexander because the extreme park boost (+14%) outweighs the potential risk. We will be monitoring these three closely, as a rainout would force an immediate scramble to our bench, though with **Jose Caballero** and **Giancarlo Stanton** currently sidelined on the IL, our options for late-swaps are limited. Managers should keep an eye on official lineups, as our projections for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Cole Young**, and **Tyler Soderstrom** rely on assumed batting orders that could shift these efficiency scores once the cards are posted.",
